      "model": "gpt-6-astra",
      "question": "For our transformer fleet planning, take the known failure modes for a transformer and tell me which of them we can actually watch on Transformer 1 at site MAIN with the channels it reports today. For each failure mode, list the two or three most diagnostic sensors among hydrogen, methane, ethane, ethylene, acetylene, co, co2, oxygen, nitrogen, dbds, dielectric_rigidity, interfacial_v, oti, oti_a, oti_t, ati, il1, il2, il3 and inut. Then mark each mode as 'well covered', 'partially covered' or 'blind', based on whether those sensors have data in the available window. Only list sensors that genuinely discriminate that mode, not ones that just correlate generally. If a mode's key sensor exists but has no values in the window, count the mode as blind rather than covered, and suggest which offline test or extra sample would close the gap.",
      "answer": "## Transformer 1 — MAIN\n\n**Available window: 15–19 January 2024, five daily records—not live data for today.** All 20 channels you listed have numeric values in all five records; none is empty.\n\nThe catalog contains nine known modes and is marked non-exhaustive. Coverage below means diagnostic observability, **not confirmation of a fault**. “Well covered” indicates the principal screening channels are populated; “partially covered” indicates an important diagnostic limitation.\n\n| Known failure mode | Most diagnostic channels from your list | Coverage | Interpretation / test to close the gap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Partial Discharge in Oil | `hydrogen`, `methane`, `acetylene` | well covered | Hydrogen-dominant gas patterns, interpreted against methane and acetylene, help distinguish PD from thermal faults and arcing. Electrical/acoustic PD testing confirms and localizes it. |\n| Thermal Fault — Oil Overheating | `methane`, `ethane`, `ethylene` | well covered | Relative hydrocarbon production discriminates thermal fault temperature regimes. Fresh laboratory DGA establishes current condition. |\n| Thermal Fault — Core/Winding | `ethylene`, `co`, `co2` | partially covered | Ethylene supports hot-oil thermal damage; CO/CO₂ support cellulose involvement. They cannot localize core versus winding damage. Add winding-resistance and core-insulation tests during an outage. |\n| Low-Energy Arcing (Sparking) | `acetylene`, `hydrogen`, `ethylene` | well covered | Joint gas patterns help distinguish discharge energy; no single gas establishes severity. Confirm with repeat laboratory DGA and electrical investigation. |\n| High-Energy Arcing | `acetylene`, `ethylene`, `hydrogen` | well covered | Acetylene with the accompanying gas pattern supports high-energy discharge screening. Gas ratios and trends—not acetylene alone—differentiate it from sparking. |\n| Tap Changer Mechanical Wear | **None sufficiently specific** | blind | Main-tank gas channels and load currents do not discriminate mechanical wear. Perform tap-changer inspection, operation timing and dynamic resistance measurements; sample its separate oil compartment where applicable. |\n| Oil Contamination / Moisture Ingress | `dielectric_rigidity`, `interfacial_v` | partially covered | Breakdown strength detects impaired oil withstand; interfacial tension supports polar contamination. Neither identifies moisture specifically. Obtain Karl Fischer moisture and particle-count tests. |\n| Cellulose Insulation Ageing | `co`, `co2` | partially covered | Their joint trend supports cellulose degradation, but oil oxidation and operating history confound age assessment. Add oil furan/2-FAL testing; paper degree-of-polymerization testing when accessible. |\n| Oil Dielectric Degradation | `dielectric_rigidity`, `interfacial_v` | well covered | Direct breakdown-strength measurement plus interfacial tension cover dielectric deterioration and associated oil ageing. Laboratory breakdown voltage, acidity and dielectric-loss testing clarify cause. |\n\n**Selection limits:** I have not forced two sensors onto tap-changer wear where none genuinely discriminates it. `dbds` targets corrosive-sulfur risk, not generic contamination; oxygen/nitrogen, temperature alarms and load currents do not independently distinguish these catalog modes. Here `interfacial_v` is interpreted as oil interfacial tension.\n\n**Planning implication:** Refresh the samples before treating this as current fleet coverage. No empty-channel blind spot appears in this window; any indispensable channel with zero values would instead make its mode **blind**, regardless of its registry installation status.",
